    {
      "id": "b9e10fc0233bb01ae51d59a4d83ff37d9d109102",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 690942480,
          "type": null,
          "body": "So this will still calculate the hash for a file that can be opened but that gets an error while reading it, right?  (It should - although I'm not sure why capinfos currently reports \"(will continue anyway, checksums might be incorrect)\" if you run it on a truncated file.  What does \"incorrect\" mean here?  Does it mean \"doesn't match what it would have been if the file weren't truncated\"?  What if the problem isn't that there was a valid file that got truncated, but that the file was written incorrectly?)",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-09-30T07:18:12.765Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-09-30T07:18:12.765Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 119042324,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 4337,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "42fa5b8f04d89a7f03640ba3fccb04394e205510",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 691241660,
          "type": null,
          "body": "It won't calculate the hash for files that `wtap_file_size()` fails on, and it won't calculate the hash for files that get any error other than `WTAP_ERR_SHORT_READ`, because in those situations capinfos doesn't report _any_ information for the file now but instead returns without printing the stats, including the calculated hash.\n\nI agree that if there is a corrupt file that was written incorrectly, so that e.g. after N good read packets there is then an error that causes `WTAP_ERR_BAD_FILE`, then perhaps the stats up to that point should be printed, and the hashes calculated. That seems like useful information to put into a large table report run with `capinfos *.pcap` on a directory or similar. That seems to me like an issue for a different merge request, though, changing the [current return behavior](https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/blob/master/capinfos.c#L1339-L1354).\n\nI am not sure how to interpret the \"(will continue anyway, checksums might be incorrect)\" when run on a truncated file. The checksums are always calculated over the entire file using a different file handler, so whether they are correct or not in the larger sense depends on errors there. I suppose it does means something like \"doesn't match what it would have been if the file weren't truncated,\" and I'm not convinced that's a useful error message.\n\nCertainly good points, but they do seem like issues for a different merge request to me. I think not taking seconds (or minutes, or even hours, for very large files) to do expensive hash calculations that will never be displayed can be changed first and the error handling fixed later. Are you suggesting adding changes to the error handling to this request, though?",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
